---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import data from "../data/experience.json";

const years = new Date().getFullYear() - data.profile.careerStart;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`Software engineer in Costa Rica, ${years} years in — ERP back ends, then front-end architecture, design systems and micro frontends.`}
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Cristian Arce</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        I'm a software engineer in Costa Rica, {years} years in. I started on the
        back end of ERP systems, moved to the front end, and now lead web
        practice work for US-based clients — design systems, micro frontends,
        and the people building them.
      </p>

      <p>
        The path matters more than the labels. My first years were spent inside
        Exactus ERP — point-of-sales, electronic invoicing, CRM — for clients
        across Central America and the Caribbean. That work was unglamorous and
        it taught me the thing I still lean on: software that handles somebody's
        money is judged on correctness long before it's judged on anything else.
        Later, building full-stack for US clients, I spent as much time in
        stored procedures and query plans as in the UI.
      </p>

      <p>
        I moved to the front end because the pace of the web stack held my
        attention, and I stayed because the interesting problems moved there
        too. Design systems, micro frontends, and the integration seams between
        them are where most of my recent work has been — the parts where a
        decision made once gets paid for, or paid off, by every team downstream.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>
        I care about the reasoning behind a stack more than the stack itself.
        Most technical choices are defensible; what separates them is whether
        anyone can reconstruct why the choice was made two years later. That's
        the whole premise of the <a href="/work">case studies</a> on this site —
        the decision and the rejected alternative, not a list of logos.
      </p>

      <p>
        The other half of the job is people. A good part of my time goes to
        mentorship, career-path conversations, and evaluating candidates, and
        it's the part I'd least want to give up. It comes from my own time as a
        student: <em>I teach others the way I wish I had been taught.</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        Right now my learning time is going into AI — as a student of it, not as
        someone claiming production experience yet. When that changes, it'll
        show up here as a case study rather than a line in a skills list.
      </p>

      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <ul class="links">
        <li><a href="mailto:crisarji@gmail.com">crisarji@gmail.com</a></li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/crisarji" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/cristian-arce-a0757158/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            LinkedIn <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="aside">
        Email is the reliable one. The <a href="/experience">experience map</a>
        has the full history, and <a href="/resume">/resume</a> is the same thing
        as plain text.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem) clamp(3rem, 8vw, 5rem); }
  h1 { margin-top: 0.5rem; }
  .prose { margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .lede { font-size: var(--t-lg); }
  h2 { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .links { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .links li + li { margin-top: 0.35rem; }
  .aside {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
